      {
         "id": "b",
         "prompt": "Find \\( \\displaystyle\\int q(x)\\,dx \\). Use your result to evaluate \\( \\displaystyle\\int_{2}^{3} q(x)\\,dx \\).",
         "setup_required": false,
         "answer_latex": "1 + 4\\ln 2 - \\ln 3",
         "worked_solution": [
            {
               "text": "The degrees are equal, so divide first.",
               "latex": "\\frac{x^{2} + 3x}{x^{2} - 1} = 1 + \\frac{3x + 1}{(x - 1)(x + 1)}"
            },
            {
               "text": "Clear denominators: 3x + 1 = A(x + 1) + B(x - 1). At x = 1, 4 = 2A; at x = -1, -2 = -2B.",
               "latex": "q(x) = 1 + \\frac{2}{x - 1} + \\frac{1}{x + 1}"
            },
            {
               "text": "Antidifferentiate.",
               "latex": "\\int q(x)\\,dx = x + 2\\ln|x - 1| + \\ln|x + 1| + C"
            },
            {
               "text": "Evaluate from 2 to 3.",
               "latex": "\\left(3 + 2\\ln 2 + \\ln 4\\right) - \\left(2 + 2\\ln 1 + \\ln 3\\right) = 1 + 4\\ln 2 - \\ln 3"
            }
